    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "In 1883, a single sound ruptured eardrums forty miles away. Sailors nearby watched air pressure spike until barometers shattered.",
        "visual_keywords": "stormy ocean old ship"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Four hours later, people three thousand miles away heard artillery. Across the Indian Ocean, coastlines shook from an invisible blast.",
        "visual_keywords": "tropical island coastline waves"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Then, barometers in London and San Francisco began spiking hours apart. How could one sound hit every continent over and over?",
        "visual_keywords": "vintage barometer antique clock"
      },
      {
        "narration": "It was the eruption of Krakatoa, exploding with two hundred megatons of force. The blast hurled a massive pressure wave through the atmosphere.",
        "visual_keywords": "volcano erupting dark smoke"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The pressure wave circled the Earth three and a half times.",
        "visual_keywords": "planet earth from space"
      }
